v4l2_codec2: an ignored capture buffer does not fetch a new frame

When the driver hands back a capture buffer that is not at the device
(0027 drops it), the loop still marked an output buffer as freed and
posted tryFetchVideoFrame. Before the first resolution change there is
no frame pool yet, so that failed the decoder ("mVideoFramePool is
null"). YouTube restarts its decoder whenever it comes back to the
front, hit this about once per switch, failed playback and started a
new decoder, sometimes showing a green frame on the way. Only a buffer
that was at the device now counts as freed.

diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
index 2d6bd13..6caeb60 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
@@ -686,8 +686,6 @@ void V4L2Decoder::serviceDeviceTask(bool event) {
         }
         if (!dequeuedBuffer) break;
 
-        outputDequeued = true;
-
         const size_t bufferId = dequeuedBuffer->bufferId();
         const int32_t bitstreamId = static_cast<int32_t>(dequeuedBuffer->getTimeStamp().tv_sec);
         const size_t bytesUsed = dequeuedBuffer->getPlaneBytesUsed(0);
@@ -704,6 +702,11 @@ void V4L2Decoder::serviceDeviceTask(bool event) {
             ALOGW("Ignoring output buffer %zu that is not at the device", bufferId);
             continue;
         }
+        // Only a buffer that was at the device frees one for a new frame. An
+        // ignored one can come before the first resolution change, when there
+        // is no frame pool yet: fetching a frame then failed the decoder, and
+        // the app (YouTube coming back to the front) had to start it again.
+        outputDequeued = true;
         auto frame = std::move(it->second);
         mFrameAtDevice.erase(it);
 
